665692271,2026-10-03 04:45:55,Coolday007,2022-12-26,10,0,,$CRWV FACTUAL NEWS * AWS GPU rental prices: Amazon said it will raise prices by roughly 15% next week for EC2 Capacity Blocks using Nvidia GPUs ranging from the older A100 to the newer B300. This is the A100’s first increase since January; AWS also raised some GPU-reservation prices in July. The change applies to this particular reservation product—not necessarily every AWS GPU-rental option. TRADING INTERPRETATION * Opinion — bullish for NVDA and CRWV; supportive for MU: AWS raising prices on both old and new Nvidia capacity is concrete evidence that scarce AI compute retains pricing power. The A100 increase is particularly encouraging because it suggests older GPUs are not rapidly becoming worthless. * CoreWeave implication: This supports CoreWeave’s claim that contracted compute prices can rise even while newer GPU generations arrive. All compute made will always be used. This has been this way since the first computer was made.
665692384,2026-10-03 05:01:14,Coolday007,2022-12-26,10,0,,"$CRWV Yes — so far, several important parts of Michael Burry’s AI thesis look wrong, or at least premature. The strongest example is the one you and I have been discussing: older GPUs. Burry argued that the economic life of AI chips could be much shorter than the 5–6 year depreciation schedules companies use. But A100s from 2020 are still being rented and generating revenue, and CoreWeave has contracts involving A100 capacity extending through 2029. Even Burry acknowledges that Michael Intrator says CoreWeave’s A100s remain fully booked and that some older H100 capacity has been re-leased at very high rates. That matters because one of Burry’s central arguments was essentially: rapidly improving chips → older chips lose economic value quickly → depreciation is understated → AI profits are overstated. What’s actually happening is closer to what you’ve been arguing: the newest GPUs become the premium product, while older GPUs move down the performance ladder but remain useful"
665692423,2026-10-03 05:07:31,Coolday007,2022-12-26,10,0,,"$CRWV Even if interest rates rise to 6%, that does not automatically break the AI infrastructure economics. Financing would become more expensive, but compute pricing is moving higher too. Over the recent 90-day period, H200 rental prices rose about 27%, H200 NVL about 30%, and B200 about 73%, while H100 SXM rose about 6%. The question isn’t whether rates rise—it’s whether the cost of financing rises faster than the revenue and margins generated by the compute. So far, high-end compute pricing has been moving much faster than interest rates."
665692465,2026-10-03 05:14:07,Coolday007,2022-12-26,10,2,,"$CRWV Here’s the concise CoreWeave positive list from roughly the past week: * JPMorgan upgraded CRWV from Neutral to Overweight, raising its target from $120 to $125, citing stronger compute pricing and improving margins. * UBS initiated Buy with a $120 target, arguing AI infrastructure demand remains strong. * William Blair initiated Outperform on Sept. 30. * Rosenblatt reiterated Buy/$250, specifically pointing to CoreWeave’s pricing power. * Cantor maintained Overweight/$176 on Oct. 2. * Compute pricing is rising: JPMorgan cited roughly 25% price increases earlier this year; Rosenblatt says CoreWeave recently added another ~10% increase. * Older GPUs are staying economically useful: CoreWeave is selling three-year access to A100s, while its existing A100 contracts extend into 2029. That directly weakens the rapid-GPU-obsolescence argument."
