666242168,2026-10-11 14:29:37,10xorbust,2025-10-03,416,6,Bullish,"$APLD Yes, I’ve read what’s public as part of my DD. Only the $CRWV leases have been publicly disclosed and filed with the SEC although of course they are in redacted form. The contents of the other leases have been disclosed in $APLD filings in terms of the characterizations of them. And yes, tenants CAN terminate the leases IF $APLD misses the delivery deadlines in the contracts. $CRWV and $ORCL both have that protection. There are grace periods, possible extensions and notice requirements, so missing a target date doesn’t automatically give the tenant a way out. For $CRWV’s first two leases, $APLD specifically says a qualifying delivery failure can let the tenant terminate the lease without a termination fee or further lease payments. For the remaining 150 MW building, there is a July 29, 2027 deadline for the first three halls to meet the required commencement conditions. Some of the details are redacted, so I wouldn’t pretend we can see every exception. For $ORCL, there is a cancellation right if delivery runs more than 150 days late against phased dates starting October 1, 2026. Fitch also reports an $ORCL parent guarantee and rights to terminate for chronic outages. But $ORCL’s actual agreements are not public, so I don’t know every notice requirement or exactly what $ORCL would owe when using one of those exits. For the $META projects at Delta Forge 1, Delta Forge 2 and Polaris Forge 3, I’ve read the public disclosures. I haven’t seen the actual tenant leases which are NOT available. To be clear, $APLD has never acknowledged $META as the tenant but Wells Fargo reported it, and I have reported it too from mine and other’s examinations of the publicly available filings. ￼ We know $APLD acknowledges construction-delay cancellation risk, but we don’t have those projects’ specific deadlines, exceptions or guarantee terms. I’m not going to claim I’ve reviewed clauses that haven’t been released. If a tenant simply changes its plans, that’s expensive. APLD says voluntary cancellation by the tenant requires payment of the entire remaining contract value. $CRWV’s clause is explicit: 100% of the remaining scheduled base rent, plus specified taxes, paid before the cancellation takes effect. That payment requirement is separate from its rights to terminate the lease for delivery failures, uncured $APLD defaults, major property damage or condemnation. The lease values are roughly $11B for $CRWV , $5B for $ORCL and $20.2B across the $META projects, over 15 years. Most have three optional five-year renewals; $ORCL has two. I count the roughly $36B of initial-term commitments separately from the much larger $85 billion figure that assumes every renewal gets exercised. There are also protections in the later $CRWV arrangements. Some leases moved to a subsidiary, with parent guarantees that kick in under specified circumstances. At ELN-02, if the subsidiary’s lease ends, the suspended parent lease resumes for those halls. Moving the lease into a subsidiary doesn’t, by itself, make the parent’s commitment disappear. As we all know, the KEY factor to $APLD raking in this huge pile of revenue is on time construction execution. So far, $APLD has executed its construction milestones flawlessly. There’s no guarantee $APLD will continue to do that but I’m quite optimistic given it’s spotless to date track record. $APLD already has the first 250 MW for $CRWV operating at Polaris Forge 1 and has projected completing another 600 MW within the next 12 months. These lease contracts make it expensive for tenants to change their minds, but $APLD still must finish the remaining buildings on time and keep them running as promised. I take the backlog seriously. The delivery deadlines are part of the deal too."
