666795455,2026-10-18 14:30:34,KavaStocks,2021-02-03,6822,24,,"$APLD $CRWV $META Meta reports Wednesday, and Applied Digital and CoreWeave both have a lot riding on it. The APLD board has speculated that Meta is the customer behind the company&#39;s three newest campuses and offered some great evidence for it. The filings check out. The tenant on the Delta Forge 2 county paperwork is registered at 1 Meta Way. Applied has already said all three campuses share one customer, so more than half of its contract book would sit with Meta. It&#39;s still unofficial, which is how hyperscalers tend to like it. Meta matters on the CoreWeave side, too. It&#39;s one of CoreWeave&#39;s biggest customers, and a Meta contract backed the first investment-grade loan CoreWeave ever landed. Applied&#39;s credit work on the Ellendale leases came right behind that loan. On that side of the book, CoreWeave is the middleman. This week the market went after the middleman, and the backdrop gave it plenty of reason. The Fed went back to hiking in September, Wednesday&#39;s inflation print ran hot, and the odds of a second hike at the October 28 meeting went from about one in five to roughly a coin flip. Money rotated out of AI and chips, which is how the Dow finished the week up while the Nasdaq pulled back a week after it set a record. Oracle, the Korean memory names, and the neoclouds all sold off on worries about how the buildout gets financed and what that money costs. CoreWeave&#39;s unsecured 2031s traded as low as 78 cents on the dollar, yielding more than 16 percent, while the 10-year fell almost a quarter of a point. Rising hike odds and a falling 10-year read to me like a market pricing a Fed that tightens into a slowdown, which is a bad combination for the biggest borrower in the group. Applied&#39;s exposure runs through Ellendale. CoreWeave is the weakest credit on a tenant list where everyone else is investment grade, and its bear case was always going to live in the interest line. Last quarter that interest bill ran about five times its adjusted operating income. CoreWeave will be back in the market for money for at least the next couple of years, and the cheapest door it has left depends on rating agencies feeling generous in a tape like this. I&#39;m not waving that off. Then I pulled up the landlord paper, which was a lot calmer. The 7 percent notes from June, the ones funding the third Ellendale building, slipped about a point to 94.5. CoreWeave is the tenant behind those notes and the borrower behind its own 2031s, yet at the lows their yields were about eight percentage points apart. Most of that gap is collateral. The Ellendale noteholders have a lien on the building and its lease, and CoreWeave needs that capacity to serve its own customers. CoreWeave&#39;s unsecured holders get paid after everyone with collateral. Cipher&#39;s secured notes, with no CoreWeave behind them, barely moved off par. That tells me the people who get paid first are pricing this as a CoreWeave problem. The stocks didn&#39;t sort any of it out. Landlords with no CoreWeave exposure got sold right alongside Applied and Galaxy, and I&#39;d have been surprised by anything else. The landlords themselves stand about where their bonds do. If I got to pick how a credit scare in this sector plays out, I&#39;d pick something close to this week, with the most levered name&#39;s unsecured debt taking the hit while the paper secured by buildings mostly holds. The risk I&#39;m left with sits in the stretch where interest is already on the books and the rent is still catching up. A building slipping now gives a tenant under pressure an opening to revisit the lease, and the financing for the next campuses gets priced in this tape. If the landlord notes start following CoreWeave&#39;s unsecured debt down, the structure isn&#39;t providing the protection I&#39;m counting on, and the bond quotes would show it well before a lease does. Meta goes first on Wednesday. If the spending holds, the money keeps moving down the line. A capex cut or softer talk about renting outside capacity would hit everyone below it, and Applied twice. The Fed meets a week later, CoreWeave reports a couple of weeks after that, and I&#39;ll read its interest line before I look at the backlog. It&#39;s an uncomfortable spot to be in. The parts of the structure I can check held up this week, and I own the equity, which is last in line behind all of it. The stock and the structure can stay out of sync for a while, and this kind of selloff will probably return every time CoreWeave raises money. I can live with that as long as the landlord notes stay where they are."
