666883554,2026-10-19 20:16:18,mydoginvests,2018-01-03,68,1,,"$CRWV Fair question, and the honest answer is: it&#39;s not quite a pump-and-dump, but the conflict you&#39;re smelling is real. Here&#39;s the mechanics. **Goldman isn&#39;t a neutral observer here.** I checked: Goldman Sachs is a sales agent on CoreWeave&#39;s $2.9B at-the-market equity program from September — meaning Goldman literally earns fees selling CoreWeave shares into the market, up to 35 million of them, whenever CoreWeave decides to. Goldman was also a bookrunner on the $8.5B investment-grade loan facility and the August credit agreement. So when Goldman&#39;s analyst upgrades the stock, it directly helps the machine Goldman itself gets paid by: higher stock price, better prices on the shares they&#39;re hired to sell, happier banking client. That&#39;s all disclosed in the filings — it&#39;s legal, analysts certify their views are independent — but the incentives all point one direction. Banks almost never trash their own fee-paying clients."
